# Function simplicity and branching

- Prefer **short, linear** functions with **low cyclomatic complexity**.
- **Many distinct conditions**, **deeply nested `if` / `else`**, or **long switch** blocks are a signal to **extract helpers** (boolean predicates, small pure functions, or dedicated steps) instead of growing one function.

## When to refactor

- **3+ meaningful branches** that each do real work → consider **named helpers** or early returns with **guard clauses** in tiny functions.
- **Nesting more than 1–2 levels** for business logic → **flatten** by extracting branches into functions with descriptive names.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: many nested branches in one function
function validate(user: User): boolean {
  if (user) {
    if (user.email) {
      if (user.email.includes("@")) {
        if (user.age >= 18) {
          if (!user.suspended) return true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return false;
}

// ✅ GOOD: guard clauses (extract further into helpers if logic grows)
function validate(user: User): boolean {
  if (!user?.email?.includes("@")) return false;
  if (user.age < 18) return false;
  if (user.suspended) return false;
  return true;
}
```

Use **early returns** for invalid input, then keep the happy path readable. Match **existing patterns** in the same module when splitting code.
